<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Verde</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape Verde is an archipelago in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, uninhabited until Portuguese settlement in the fifteenth century. It became an important base for Atlantic shipping and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. The islands remained a colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> until independence in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-verde.jpg" alt="Cape Verde" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Pedro Gregório Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cape_Verde_(3-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Portuguese language became, and remains, a major world language with more than 250 million speakers, used officially in Brazil, Angola, Mozambique, <b>Cape Verde</b>, Guinea-Bissau, São Tomé and Príncipe, East Timor and Macau, perhaps the empire’s most tangible legacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain ratified the Treaty of Tordesillas, signed on 7 June, which divided newly discovered lands outside Europe between Portugal and Castile along a meridian 370 leagues west of the <b>Cape Verde</b> islands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The two kingdoms divided newly found lands along a meridian 370 leagues west of <b>Cape Verde</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-7">7 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The uninhabited <b>Cape Verde</b> islands were reached by navigators in Portuguese service between 1456 and 1462.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-cape-verde">Portuguese Cape Verde</a></li><li><blockquote>The colony was separated from <b>Cape Verde</b> in 1879, and military campaigns between 1912 and 1915 imposed control over the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a></li><li><blockquote>Portugal granted independence to Guinea-Bissau, Mozambique, Angola, <b>Cape Verde</b> and São Tomé in 1974-1975, receiving hundreds of thousands of returnees.</blockquote><a href="/state/third-portuguese-republic">Third Portuguese Republic</a></li></ul></section>
